WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:04.020
So we haven't checked in for a little bit
about what's going on with Corona.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.150
So I want to spend a little time today.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.150
It's been a weekend.

00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:08.580
We have another several days of data.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.830
We now have more averages and trends to look
at.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:14.049
And I want to go a little deeper than just
cases and deaths.

00:00:14.049 --> 00:00:15.950
But let's start there.

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:21.750
As of this morning, the United States had
more than 5.2 million confirmed corona virus

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:26.140
cases and just shy of 166 thousand deaths.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:32.169
Now, remember that in terms of deaths, Donald
Trump said through April and May.

00:00:32.169 --> 00:00:35.320
First, he said 15 cases will soon be zero.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.510
That didn't work out in April, May.

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:41.110
Donald Trump said, well, we could end up with
50 or 60000 deaths.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:43.520
Then it became clear it was gonna be more.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.350
Trump moved the goalpost to seventy eighty
thousand ninety one hundred thousand.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.789
Now it's about keeping it under hundred thousand.

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:53.100
Now, the official death toll at 166.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.370
Later in the show, I'm going to talk to you
about the actual death toll.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:05.089
Now really is probably more like 225 because
of the uncounted corona virus deaths.

00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:06.250
Let me not get ahead of myself.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:07.740
We'll get to that a little bit later.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:08.740
But OK.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.020
That were officially at one hundred and sixty
six thousand.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:17.650
Now, in terms of cases per capita, the states
now at the top of the list are Louisiana,

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:23.549
Arizona, Florida, New York, Mississippi, New
Jersey, Alabama, Georgia, South Carolina,

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:24.770
Rhode Island and Nevada.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:29.610
And what you're seeing is that what early
in terms of per capita cases were exclusively

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.939
these blue state hotspots.

00:01:31.939 --> 00:01:38.049
As a result of which states have the direct
flights to the source contagion regions, there's

00:01:38.049 --> 00:01:43.350
been a shift to red states, not just in the
total number of cases, but in cases per capita,

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:44.820
because now it's about policy.

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:50.939
Early it was about who had the bad luck or
timing is what I guess I would call it, too,

00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:57.579
before we really knew about the virus, have
a lot of direct flights to China and the source

00:01:57.579 --> 00:01:59.109
countries in Europe like Italy.

00:01:59.109 --> 00:02:04.710
And it was, you know, Boston, New York, New
Jersey area, Chicago, Illinois, the California

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:05.710
airports.

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:09.179
Rhode Island, by virtue of being very close
to Boston, also was hit very hard.

00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:16.130
Now it's about who's testing, who has guidelines,
where are people wearing masks, et cetera.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:23.690
So now it's about policy cases, of course,
depend on tests and how much testing you are

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:24.690
doing.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:29.170
And as we know, as I told you last week, Florida
and California as examples were under reporting

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:30.819
cases for different reasons.

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:32.090
Florida because of the hurricane.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.790
California for still some unknown reason.

00:02:34.790 --> 00:02:38.760
So when you look at one of these examples,
let's look at, for example, Florida, because

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:45.680
of the decreased testing in Florida, Florida
cases started declining in late July, except

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.459
we know why they aren't testing as much because
of the hurricane.

00:02:49.459 --> 00:02:56.879
But if you look instead of at cases, at hospitalizations,
during the last week, Florida had a record

00:02:56.879 --> 00:03:01.959
for single week, new hospitalizations for
Corona virus, just over 30, 300.

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:09.909
That's more than 500 new hospitalizations
per day, including 621 hospitalizations in

00:03:09.909 --> 00:03:11.890
Florida last Wednesday.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:13.470
That's not what you want to see.

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:21.299
And it's another signal of how you can affect
cases by not testing, by changing reporting.

00:03:21.299 --> 00:03:23.510
Hospitalizations are a really important indicator.

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:29.420
Now, meanwhile, for political reasons, Donald
Trump is talking about every country now is

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:30.680
seeing a resurgence.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:35.180
And the reason Trump is doing this is if he
can point to Spain is having a wave to Italy

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:36.849
is having a wave, too.

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:40.629
I guess he thinks that makes the United States
response look less bad.

00:03:40.629 --> 00:03:43.650
But there's a few really important things
to think about as we look at some European

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:46.200
countries, see numbers start to climb again.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:50.280
First of all, we've been expecting a second
wave since March.

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:54.920
This was what doctors thought would happen,
decrease in the summer, back up as we approach

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.950
fall in the U.S.

00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:02.760
We never even had the gap because when we
got down to 20000 cases per day, most states

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.469
started reopening, which is crazy.

00:04:05.469 --> 00:04:07.060
Now to the other piece.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:08.060
Yes.

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:13.459
If you look at some European countries now
like Spain, they are seeing a resurgence in

00:04:13.459 --> 00:04:18.829
cases, but they're immediately putting the
guidelines back in place and people are mostly

00:04:18.829 --> 00:04:19.870
following them.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:25.270
So when the president points to we're doing
fine because there are second waves in Europe.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:30.759
Well, first reaction is we knew and expected
all along that there would be a second wave

00:04:30.759 --> 00:04:31.900
in the U.S.

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:33.419
We've been so acutely.

00:04:33.419 --> 00:04:38.400
Terrible that we didn't even get a break from
wave one to wave to.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.810
And we're now approaching the fall ourselves.

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:44.220
Secondly, the other countries see the rise.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.240
And they put the guidelines back in place.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.699
Paris is now ramping back up, masking rules
and putting measures back in place.

00:04:50.699 --> 00:04:55.620
Trump points to Spain and France to justify
not having taken it seriously.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:01.500
But the problem with his logic is they didn't
take it seriously and immediately are starting

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:02.500
to right away.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:07.460
It's completely incoherent, but it's where
we are today and all we can do is deal with

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.370
it real quick.

